syncd - commit the filesystem's held-back changes on a timer

SYNOPSIS
    syncd

DESCRIPTION
    The filesystem server batches metadata changes into a journal
    transaction rather than committing one per operation, which is what
    keeps the disk traffic down. A batch is committed when it fills up,
    when somebody calls sync, or when it is more than a second old and
    another request arrives.

    That last condition needs another request. A burst of writes followed
    by an idle filesystem left the batch sitting there indefinitely,
    because the server is asleep in ipc_recv with nothing to wake it and
    no clock of its own to wake it with.

    syncd is that clock. It sleeps five seconds and calls sync, forever.
    It is started by init and takes no arguments.

    It could have been a thread inside the server. The server is
    single-threaded on purpose - one request at a time, and so no locking
    around the transaction buffer, the mount table or the block cache -
    and a second thread in there touching all three would be a large
    change to buy a small guarantee. From outside it is an ordinary
    client and the guarantee is the same.

    Five seconds is what ext4 uses, and the trade is the same one: a crash
    loses at most that much work, and an idle machine writes its disk
    occasionally rather than never. It is the job `update` did on early
    UNIX.

BUGS
    A commit still cannot be proven to have reached the platter. The
    driver has no flush this can wait on, so this makes the window
    bounded rather than making it airtight.

SEE ALSO
    sync, fsck, mount
